From ee0951b69454494587c6718f233e37e5463fbcb2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: amd-weisun <Wei.Sun@amd.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:01:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Let the FP8 paged decode take any block-table padding,
 and test it

paged_attention_decode_fp8 (FlyDSL, gfx950) loads a whole 256-token tile of
block-table entries and masks the tokens past each sequence's context only
after reading their pages. Slots of the table past a sequence's pages are
padding, which callers may fill with anything (the other FlyDSL paged decodes
are tested with -1 there), but this kernel used them as page indices: a
padding entry outside the cache faults the GPU (memory aperture violation).
The table itself was read through an unbounded descriptor, so a table with no
columns (all sequences empty) faulted too, and the last row's padding entries
could be read past the end of the allocation.

The kernel now replaces entries past the context with block 0 when it stages
them, as zero padding did, and bounds its reads to the table's rows (a table
with no columns gets stride 0, so its reads return 0). The cost is within
noise (11.9 -> 12.0 us at batch 1, 1k context; others unchanged). The
launcher also rejects context lengths and block tables that are not int32,
which the kernel misread silently (int64 lengths gave later sequences length 0).

The op had no tests. The new pa_decode_fp8_test.py compares it with an fp32
reference over the dequantized cache, on ragged lengths (including empty
sequences and ones shorter than a page), block sizes 16 and 64, head sizes 64,
128 and 256, group sizes 1 to 16, f16 and bf16 queries, -1 padding, a table
with no columns, int64 indices, and a CUDA graph replayed with new lengths and pages. On the
old kernel the second case faults. Besides an elementwise tolerance wide
enough for FP8 rounding, each sequence must match the reference in scale
(within 2.5%) and in relative L2 error (within 10%), which catches K or V
scale errors of a few percent. fp8_paged_cache.py gains the random cache
builder and dequantizer the test uses. ROCm CI skips test/attention/ unless a
file is enabled explicitly, so the new file is.

